{
  // oxlint, adopted as a RATCHET on 2026-09-19. Read this before adding a rule.
  //
  // Three categories are enforced as errors. Every rule in them is on unless it
  // is listed below, so a rule oxlint adds in a future version arrives enforced
  // rather than silently off. **That holds inside the five plugins named below
  // and nowhere else**, which is the price of naming a list at all; the census
  // in `oxlintRatchet.test.ts` is what keeps the other nine from being an
  // accident. `pedantic`, `style` and `restriction` are not enforced: measured
  // the day this landed, they produce 2053, 25604 and 6291 findings over `src`
  // and `tests`, which is a rewrite rather than a ratchet.
  //
  // THE SUPPRESSION LIST IS NOT ONE THING. It has two halves and they age
  // differently: a refusal is permanent and carries the reason it is a refusal,
  // and a backlog entry is a rule this tree would pass if somebody did the work.
  // Adding to the backlog half is how this ratchet stops being one, so a new
  // entry needs a count and a reason in the commit that adds it.
  //
  // `rules` opens with something that is NEITHER, and it is not a third kind of
  // suppression: it is configuration, a rule left ON with an option, and it
  // belongs with `plugins` and `categories` rather than with the two halves. It
  // sits inside `rules` only because that is the key oxlint takes a rule's
  // options under. Keep "the list" meaning the suppressions below it.
  "ignorePatterns": ["src/api/generated/**"],

  // NAMING A PLUGIN LIST REPLACES THE DEFAULT SET. It does not add to it, and
  // nothing warns. oxlint's default here is `unicorn`, `typescript` and `oxc`,
  // which is asked of `oxlint --print-config` rather than read off a document,
  // so a list that forgets one of those three turns that plugin off across the
  // whole tree in silence. Driven on a file carrying one duplicate enum value
  // and one duplicate object key: under `plugins: ["vitest"]` the typescript
  // rule stops firing and the core rule still does, because the core rules are
  // not a plugin in this sense and are always on. That is the opposite of what
  // this file is for, so the three defaults are named back here.
  //
  // Naming them back reproduces the default set exactly. Measured with
  // `pedantic`, `style` and `restriction` switched on so that tens of thousands
  // of findings were in play, over the same 487 files: the two sets of whole
  // diagnostics differ by NOTHING IN EITHER DIRECTION, three repetitions. The
  // point total is deliberately not written here. Two seats derived it and got
  // 34353 and 34349, each deterministic over three runs of its own, and neither
  // could account for the four; the empty difference is the claim that carries
  // the argument and it is the one both readings agree on.
  //
  // `react` and `vitest` are the two added. Neither is a default, so neither
  // had ever run here, `react` in a React 19 application. Between them they put
  // 64 more rules into the three categories below, 203 against 157 for react
  // and 175 against 157 for vitest. **Eight of the 64 are turned off below**,
  // the permanent react refusal and the seven backlog rows; every other one is
  // live and finds nothing today, so they guard from the moment this lands.
  // The configured rule is among the live ones and is clean BECAUSE of its
  // option, not in spite of it: under the default it has findings, and the
  // bound takes it to zero.
  //
  // Count the rows rather than subtracting. The subtraction that stood here
  // was one out, and it put the configured rule among the rules with findings
  // while the paragraph two screens down said the option takes it to none.
  //
  // RECOUNT WITH THIS, which is where every figure in this file came from:
  //
  //     ./node_modules/.bin/oxlint src tests --format json
  //
  // and read `number_of_rules` and `number_of_files` off the report, plus the
  // `code` of each diagnostic for a per rule count. It reads 172 rules over 487
  // files here, against 116 before this landed.
  //
  // **The `lint` script prints nothing at all, so do not look there.** The
  // implicit renderer and the named default renderer are different renderers:
  // with no format flag the run is silent on both streams, clean or dirty, and
  // only with the renderer named explicitly do the two numbers appear, on
  // standard output. `package.json` passes no flag. That is why the recount
  // above goes through the structured report rather than through the command
  // anybody actually types.
  //
  // oxlint ships fourteen plugins. `oxlintRatchet.test.ts` carries the other
  // nine by name, each with the reason it is not here and what naming it would
  // cost, and refuses a tenth that a future version adds with nothing said
  // about it. That is where the scope limit noted at the top is held.
  "plugins": ["typescript", "unicorn", "oxc", "react", "vitest"],
  "categories": {
    "correctness": "error",
    "suspicious": "error",
    "perf": "error"
  },
  "rules": {
    // ---- CONFIGURATION, not a suppression. The rule stays ON. ----
    //
    // Read the header: this is here because oxlint takes a rule's options under
    // `rules`, not because it is a third kind of suppression. The two halves
    // begin below it.

    // The default bound is one argument and that is jest's. vitest's `expect`
    // takes a second argument, the message the failure prints, and this tree
    // uses that form: under the default every finding is that one form, across
    // 17 files, with no other message among them, and the option takes the tree
    // to 0.
    //
    // No total is written here on purpose. The count moves whenever anybody
    // writes another two argument assertion, including in the test file that
    // guards this entry, which is itself inside the linted tree: the figure
    // that stood here was already stale in the commit that corrected it.
    //
    // Two is the signature's own bound rather than a round number chosen to
    // clear the tree, which is what makes this configuration and not a dressed
    // up suppression: three arguments are still refused.
    //
    // IT COSTS NO COVERAGE HERE, AND THAT IS MEASURED RATHER THAN ASSERTED. An
    // option can cost coverage, so the test for one is to plant the default's
    // own cases and check they survive. Planted: four expects, one per shape.
    // The default reports three of them, "at most 1 argument", "requires at
    // least 1 argument" and "must have a corresponding matcher call". At
    // `maxArgs` 2 it reports the same three, the first reading "at most 2". So
    // the only thing that moved is the bound. Suppressing the rule would have
    // thrown all three away, including the assertion that asserts nothing,
    // which is the single most valuable thing the test plugin brings.
    "vitest/valid-expect": ["error", { "maxArgs": 2 }],

    // ---- Refused, permanently. The rule is wrong about this codebase. ----

    // 47 sites. Every bulk write here is sequential BY DESIGN: a 300 book batch
    // would otherwise open 300 concurrent requests against one SQLite writer.
    // `lib/bulkWrite.ts` exists to own that rule and `docs/decisions.md` records
    // it twice. Enabling this rule would flag the architecture.
    "no-await-in-loop": "off",

    // 2 sites. In `lib/safeHref.ts` the U+0000 to U+0020 character class IS
    // the URL sanitisation logic, so the rule fires on the security control
    // itself. In `lib/pdf.ts` a second class strips the control characters a
    // PDF producer pads its metadata with, which is the point of that
    // function.
    //
    // **The second site is masked by an inline disable directive and this
    // entry said one site for as long as that was true.** So the count read as
    // one easy fix: turn the rule on, repair `safeHref.ts`, and leave a second
    // site silently suppressed by a comment. The directive census in
    // `oxlintRatchet.test.ts` is what stops the next one hiding.
    "no-control-regex": "off",

    // 1 site, `lib/sqlite.ts`: a zero width space inside a docstring's glob
    // pattern, so that the star slash does not close the comment.
    "no-irregular-whitespace": "off",

    // 1 site, `lib/bookBounds.ts`. The rule is RIGHT: 9223372036854775807 is
    // SQLite's max int64 and JS rounds it to ...808. It stays because the value
    // is a declaration mirroring the backend's bound rather than arithmetic, and
    // no collection id can reach it. If that stops being true this is a defect,
    // not a suppression.
    "no-loss-of-precision": "off",

    // 2694 sites, and the rule is WRONG about this tree rather than unpaid. It
    // requires React in scope for JSX, which the automatic runtime removed.
    // `tsconfig.json` sets `"jsx": "react-jsx"`, so the compiler supplies the
    // factory and no component imports React to render; the one file that
    // imports it, `src/main.tsx`, does so for `StrictMode`. Satisfying this
    // rule would mean adding 2694 imports the build does not want. It must
    // never be fixed, and it is here rather than in the backlog for that
    // reason: there is no work behind it.
    "react/react-in-jsx-scope": "off",

    // ---- Backlog. Counts measured 2026-09-19 over `src` and `tests`. ----
    // Each is a rule this tree could pass. None was triaged, deliberately: the
    // wave that adopted oxlint was told not to, because turning a noisy rule on
    // produces findings in files other open tickets rewrite.
    "unicorn/consistent-function-scoping": "off", // 110
    "unicorn/no-array-sort": "off", // 91
    "no-shadow": "off", // 25
    "unicorn/no-array-reverse": "off", // 5
    "oxc/no-map-spread": "off", // 5
    "unicorn/prefer-set-has": "off", // 3
    "unicorn/prefer-string-starts-ends-with": "off", // 2
    "unicorn/no-useless-spread": "off", // 2
    "no-underscore-dangle": "off", // 2
    "typescript/no-extraneous-class": "off", // 1
    "oxc/no-accumulating-spread": "off", // 1
    "preserve-caught-error": "off", // 1
    "no-useless-concat": "off", // 1

    // ---- Backlog, from the two plugins named above. Counts measured
    // 2026-10-03 over the same 487 files under the same three categories. A
    // separate block rather than merged into the one above because the counts
    // were taken on a different day over a tree that has moved, and one date
    // over both would be false for half the rows. ----
    //
    // Not triaged here, on the same terms as the block above and for a sharper
    // reason: these land in test files and in components that open tickets
    // rewrite, and a plugin list is a config change. Turning any of them on is
    // a pass of its own. The three react rows under 10 are the ones to do
    // first, because each names a defect rather than a habit: 9 effects setting
    // state synchronously, 5 reads of a ref during render, and one effect
    // missing a dependency with one carrying an extra.
    //
    // The other two rows are habits: the 401 is one habit repeated across 81
    // test files, and an index used as a key is another.
    "vitest/require-mock-type-parameters": "off", // 401, over 81 files
    "react/no-array-index-key": "off", // 12
    "react/set-state-in-effect": "off", // 9
    "react/refs": "off", // 5
    "react/exhaustive-effect-dependencies": "off" // 2
  }
}
